  <document-link format="APPLICATION/PDF" url="https://redbarnet.dk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Save-the-Children-Denmark-IATI-Documents-2024-Case-1.-Coxs-Bazar.pdf">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">2023 Case Study Bangladesh: Strengthened Child Protection &amp; Education in Cox&#x2019;s Bazar: An SPA Case Study in Value for Money</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">A Value for Money study (VfM) conducted in Cox&#x2019;s Bazar show that programme funds created significant changes in norms/beliefs and increased knowledge in communities on the importance of education, safety risks and health hazards. Children participating in Learning Centers can now read, write, and count and help their parents that are illiterate. Positive improvements in wellbeing for children in their relation to teachers due to awareness on corporal punishment as well as children and parents being more caring and respectful of each other</narrative>
   </description>
   <category code="A08"></category>
   <language code="EN"></language>
   <document-date iso-date="2024-06-26"></document-date>
  </document-link>
  <document-link format="APPLICATION/PDF" url="https://redbarnet.dk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Save-the-Children-Denmark-IATI-Documents-2024-Case-3.-The-Horn-of-Africa-Region.pdf">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">2023 Case Study Horn of Africa: Voices of Youth in Policy Making Processes in Horn Of Africa</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Development of the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD) Youth Policy Framework ensure that young people in Horn of Africa have the fundamental right to actively and meaningfully engage in all matters affecting their lives and well-being. The policy provides young people with opportunities and experiences that support successful integration into society and enables them to be active members and agents of change. It further fosters consistency in the approach taken by IGAD Secretariat in addressing youth related challenges across the region.</narrative>
   </description>
   <category code="A08"></category>
   <language code="EN"></language>
   <document-date iso-date="2024-06-26"></document-date>
  </document-link>
  <document-link format="APPLICATION/PDF" url="https://redbarnet.dk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Save-the-Children-Denmark-IATI-Documents-2024-Case-2.-South-Sudan.pdf">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">2023 Case Study South Sudan: Community Participatory Budgeting Initiative in South Sudan</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Participatory Budgeting significantly improved local leadership and community engagement in the communities around the education system in Maban, South Sudan. Access to education increased through enhancement of safety measures and protection through community decisions to improve school construction, creating safe learning spaces for children. A significant change is higher level of motivation and commitment from community members. School Management Committees (SMCs) and Parent-Teacher Associations (PTAs) prioritised school activities to enhance the physical environment and to create knowledge on the importance of education for all children.</narrative>
   </description>
   <category code="A08"></category>
   <language code="EN"></language>
   <document-date iso-date="2024-06-26"></document-date>
  </document-link>
  <document-link format="APPLICATION/PDF" url="https://redbarnet.dk/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Save-the-Children-Denmark-IATI-Documents-case-2-somalia.pdf">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">2022 Case Study Somalia: Building the resilience of affected youth, children and adolescents through cash distribution and MHPSS</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Programme implemented in Baidoa showed significant increase in school enrollment. This isan accomplishment attributed to the program&#x2019;s efforts to expand access to inclusiveeducation in emergencies directed at vulnerable children. Due to hunger crisis and conflict, anincrease in internally displaced people (IDPs) migrating and settling in the community camplead to more vulnerable children being enrolled in school, increasing the number of studentsper teacher. This resulted in a decline in reading comprehension and learning in schools.</narrative>
   </description>
   <category code="A08"></category>
   <language code="EN"></language>
   <document-date iso-date="2023-06-30"></document-date>
  </document-link>
  <document-link format="APPLICATION/PDF" url="https://redbarnet.dk/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Save-the-Children-Denmark-IATI-Documents-case-3-syria.pdf">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">2022 Case Study Syria: Improving safe access to inclusive quality education and childprotection in Syria through Summer Club activities</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Summer Club educational activities increased 200 children&#x2019;s cognitive abilities to understandschool subjects and increase learning in schools by replacing traditional learning with activelearning methods. Additionally, all 200 children showed significant improvement in resilience,independence and self-confidance. Finally, an evaluation found that attending Summer Clubhad a positive impact on the children&#x2019;s wellbeing and inclusion in education services resultedin 93% of the students passing their official exams and a decrease in school dropouts.</narrative>
   </description>
   <category code="A08"></category>
   <language code="EN"></language>
